# The SPLAT Field Guide, as published.
#
#   https://claude.ai/code/artifact/18992308-da75-4782-87b8-20b6ba7b4311
#
# BEGINNING READ. The four lines at the bottom of this file answer "what is on the live page, and is
# it still current?" without opening it. Opening the artifact costs about eighteen thousand tokens of
# context and tells you nothing those lines do not: `published` and `version` say which version is
# live, `sha256` binds it to a source you already have, and `handoff` says in one line what that
# version says that the one before it did not.
#
# The comparison is made for you - DocumentationDriftTests.ThePublishedFieldGuide_IsTheSourceAsItStandsToday
# recomputes the hash over docs/SPLAT-Field-Guide.html with line endings normalised and fails when the
# source has moved ahead of the live copy. Read the page itself only to check something specific about
# how it renders, never merely to find out whether it is up to date.
#
# HANDOFF. Whoever publishes writes the `handoff` line in the same act, because the next session's
# beginning read is this file and nothing else. One line, what changed, no ceremony.
#
# The published page is a deterministic slice of docs/SPLAT-Field-Guide.html - that file minus the
# doctype/html/head/body wrapper and the two metas the artifact host supplies - so binding the source
# binds the page, and no network call is needed to do it.
#
# That drift is not hypothetical: the page sat at 511 tests, telling readers that on Windows the locks
# "do not filter network sockets", for weeks after both repository copies had been corrected to say
# that 33 of 49 templates launch with no network capability at all. Every other claim in that class is
# checked against the code. That one was checked against nothing, because the only copy a non-engineer
# actually reads lives outside this repository.
#
# The guide is republished after major changes - a census that moved, prose a reader would notice -
# and not after every commit. It stopped stating the automated test count on 2026-09-25 for exactly
# that reason: a number that changes on almost every commit was turning almost every commit into a
# manual republish of a page whose prose had not changed. The README carries the count now.
#
# WHEN THE TEST GOES RED, all three, in this order:
#
#     packaging/build-field-guide-artifact.ps1                    # writes the body to publish
#     ...publish that file to the URL above...
#     packaging/build-field-guide-artifact.ps1 -RecordPublished -Version <n> -Handoff "<one line>"
#
# The switches are separate so that recording is a deliberate act. Updating the hash without
# publishing silences the alarm and leaves the page wrong, which is worse than no check at all,
# because the page then looks verified. That is the one failure this design cannot detect, and it is
# why the last command prints what it is asserting on your behalf.
#
# Do not hand-slice the wrapper any more. The script matches whole lines; a regex for `<head` also
# matches `<header class="hero">`, and an earlier republish lost that opening tag, so the live page
# rendered its title placard as bare text for months while every other copy looked right.

published  2026-10-10
version    66
source     docs/SPLAT-Field-Guide.html
sha256     72ba67c829caf17f1d1947489b21decf0f7e3d25b93c2a3cab31e72f3d94d4d8
handoff    eight web probes moved under the Linux lease: leased 4 -> 12, Windows 54 -> 46, one in seven -> one in six; census bound and passing
